      "text": "To determine the probability that when rolling four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we can follow these steps:\n\nStep 1: Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four 6-sided dice.\nA standard 6-sided die has 6 faces, so when you roll four dice, the total number of possible outcomes is:\n\\[ 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 6^4 = 1296. \\]\n\nStep 2: Calculate the number of outcomes where all four dice show the same number.\nIf all four dice show the same number, there are exactly 6 possible outcomes (one for each number from 1 to 6).\n\nStep 3: Calculate the number of outcomes where not all four dice show the same number.\nSubtract the number of outcomes where all dice show the same number from the total number of outcomes.\n\\[ 1296 - 6 = 1290. \\]\n\nStep 4: Calculate the probability that not all four dice show the same number.\nThe probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n\\[ \\text{Probability} = \\frac{1290}{1296} = \\frac{1290 \\div 2}{1296 \\div 2} = \\frac{645}{648}. \\]\n\nStep 5: Simplify the fraction.\n\\[ \\frac{645}{648} \\text{ is already in its simplest form.} \\]\n\nSo, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{645}{648}}. \\]",
