      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number, we'll follow these steps:\n\n### Step 1: Total Possible Outcomes\nEach of the four dice has 6 faces. Therefore, the total number of possible outcomes when rolling four dice is calculated using the multiplication principle:\n\n\\[ 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 6^4 = 1296 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Favorable Outcomes (No All Same)\nWe need to count the number of outcomes where not all dice show the same number. This category includes two sub-cases: all different numbers and one number repeated.\n\n#### Sub-case 1: All Different Numbers\nFor the first die, there are 6 choices. For the second die, there are 5 remaining choices (since it must be different). For the third die, there are 4 remaining choices, and for the fourth die, there are 3 remaining choices. Thus:\n\n\\[ 6 \\times 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 = 360 \\]\n\n#### Sub-case 2: One Number Repeated\nFirst, choose which number appears most frequently (it can't be just one). There are 6 possible choices for this number. Then choose which die shows this number. There are 4 possible choices (one for each die):\n\n\\[ 6 \\times 4 = 24 \\]\n\nHowever, we have overcounted the cases where all four dice show the same number. These cases should be subtracted:\n\n\\[ 1 \\times (4-1) = 1 \\times 3 = 3 \\]\n\nSo, the number of favorable outcomes excluding these cases is:\n\n\\[ 360 + 24 - 3 = 381 \\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate Probability\nThe required probability is the ratio of favorable outcomes to total possible outcomes:\n\n\\[ \\frac{381}{1296} \\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 381 and 1296:\n\nUsing various methods, we find that the GCD is 3:\n\n\\[ \\frac{381 \\div 3}{1296 \\div 3} = \\frac{127}{432} \\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThus, the probability that when we roll four fair 6-sided dice, they won't all show the same number is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{127}{432}} \\]",
